<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Fourth Coalition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1807">1807</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Fourth Coalition (1806–1807) was fought by Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crushed the Prussian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Auerstedt in October 1806 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, then fought the Russians at Eylau and defeated them at Friedland in June 1807. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> stripped Prussia of about half its territory and created the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fourth-coalition.jpg" alt="War of the Fourth Coalition" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruedi33a · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_4th_Coalition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Fourth Coalition formed within months of the collapse of the Third. After the victory at Austerlitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sought a general peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> while trying to isolate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Several quarrels pushed Prussia towards war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, a German state in personal union with the British monarchy, had been secretly promised to Britain by Napoleon in the course of peace overtures, although it had earlier been offered to Prussia. In July 1806 Napoleon created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> under his protection, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Prussia took offence at this interference in German affairs, and on 9 October 1806 it declared war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> marched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> in three parallel columns through the Franconian Forest in his bataillon-carré formation. After skirmishes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleiz">Schleiz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>, he defeated the Prussians at the double <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jena-auerstedt">Battle of Jena–Auerstedt</a> on 14 October 1806. At Auerstedt a single corps under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Marshal Davout</a> defeated the bulk of the Prussian army. The Prussians sustained 65,000 casualties and lost a further 150,000 prisoners, while the French suffered about 15,000 casualties. Napoleon entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 27 October and issued the Berlin Decree on 21 November, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>.</p><p>The French then entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> created the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. The inconclusive and bloody Battle of Eylau on 7–8 February 1807 forced the Russians to withdraw north, and on 14 June Napoleon routed them at Friedland. Alexander I sued for peace at Tilsit. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> on 24 August 1807 after a siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> of July 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> agreed to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>. Napoleon demanded much Prussian territory, from the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, which went to the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome-bonaparte">Jérôme Bonaparte</a>, and from the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, which went to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, ruled by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Napoleon was then master of almost all western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Britain remained at war, and Napoleon's invasion of Portugal began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Fourth Coalition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Napoleon and Emperor Alexander I of Russia signed the first of the Treaties of Tilsit, ending hostilities between France and Russia in the <b>War of the Fourth Coalition</b> after the French victory at Friedland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
